top for 2.11BSD UNIX / PDP-11
===========================

Build and run
-------------
On the PDP, enter the bsd/top directory of your checkout and run:

    make
    ./top

Use an interactive terminal and an account with read access to /dev/kmem,
/dev/mem and /dev/swap, such as root. TERM must name the terminal being
used. For a VT100-compatible terminal, for example:

    TERM=vt100; export TERM
    ./top

The native build uses separate instruction and data spaces (-i) and the
2.11BSD termcap library. make produces the stripped executable top and
keeps top.debug with symbols for diagnosis. It does not install or replace
/usr/ucb/top. No curses, ncurses, /proc, Linux interfaces, or subprocesses
are used to collect or display statistics.

This implementation was originally developed on mentec at 2.11BSD patch
level 498 and optimized and tested on minerva at patch level 481. Build
against the target system's installed headers; /unix must describe its
running kernel. See PERFORMANCE.md for current results and their scope.

Reading the display
-------------------
The display follows the supplied mentec reference: hostname, time, uptime,
logged-in users and load averages; task states; CPU states; memory; swap;
a status line; a reverse-video heading; then the process rows:

    PID USER      PR  NI  TEXT  DATA STACK S     TIME   DTIME COMMAND

TIME is cumulative user + system CPU time in seconds, excluding children.
DTIME is CPU seconds since the preceding sample. The first display appears
immediately with identities, cumulative accounting and memory information.
CPU percentages say "waiting for interval sample" and DTIME is "-" until
an interval exists. Newly seen processes start with zero DTIME until they
have a preceding observation. PID and start time distinguish a reused
process-table slot from its previous occupant.

Memory columns are KiB (1024 bytes). Process segment sizes come from
64-byte kernel clicks; swap allocation uses 512-byte blocks. Used memory
includes the kernel and other allocations, so it is not the sum of the
displayed process sizes.

States are R for running/runnable, S for sleeping/waiting, T for stopped,
and Z for zombie. A following 's' means the process is swapped out. '?' in
the time fields means that its user area could not be read consistently.
Processes can exit or move while top reads memory; such entries do not
abort the monitor. Zombie exit accounting is handled separately because
the kernel reuses parts of a zombie's process structure for different data.

Terminal size
-------------
The first line uses a compact layout when the full summary will not fit.
Rows adapt to the terminal, and -n can limit the number of displayed
processes. The last column is left unused so output cannot wrap or scroll
at the right margin. Large terminals are capped at 160 columns, 50 rows
and 5000 cells to conserve the PDP-11's data address space.

SIGWINCH rebuilds the bounded screen cache after a size change. Quit,
Ctrl-C, termination and handled runtime errors restore the original
terminal settings. A terminal smaller than 40 columns by 9 rows gets a
resize notice instead of a clipped process table.

The mentec telnetd accepts NAWS (Negotiate About Window Size) but does not
ask for it automatically. With the Mac's telnet client, press Ctrl-] and
enter this at the local telnet> prompt once per connection:

    send will naws

Return to the PDP session; resizing the Mac terminal will now update the
PDP's terminal size. Without NAWS, set the size manually before running:

    stty rows 30 columns 120

Use stty size to inspect the size that the PDP actually sees.

Options and keys
----------------
    -s seconds    Refresh interval, 0.1 to 1800 seconds (default 1).
    -o column     Sort column, 0..10 (default 9, DTIME then TIME).
    -n rows       Maximum processes shown (0 fills the screen).
    -I            Initially hide idle processes.
    -h            Print command-line help.

    q             Quit.
    Space         Refresh now.
    Ctrl-L        Redraw.
    I or i        Toggle idle processes.
    P             Sort by DTIME, then TIME.
    T             Sort by cumulative TIME.
    N             Sort by PID.
    M             Sort by data size.
    s             Prompt for refresh interval (Escape cancels).
    o             Prompt for sort column (Escape cancels).
    h or ?        Toggle interactive help.

Sort columns: 0 PID, 1 numeric UID, 2 priority, 3 nice, 4 text size,
5 data size, 6 stack size, 7 state, 8 TIME, 9 DTIME, 10 command.

How the implementation works
----------------------------
A PDP-11 process has a 64 KiB data address space even when the machine has
free physical RAM. Its variables, heap and stack must share that space.
screen.c therefore keeps one bounded character image instead of two curses
screen images. It compares each new row with its previous contents and
sends only the changed span. An unchanged frame emits nothing. Output is
buffered until the frame is ready, avoiding a system call for each
character. termcap supplies the terminal's cursor and clearing sequences;
the renderer does not assume ANSI escapes.

Sampling and display need different representations. The kernel has a
fixed number of process slots, but only occupied slots need display rows.
The history array remembers each slot's last identity and CPU time. Sorts
move short indices into the display rows, avoiding repeated copies of
command names and accounting fields.

A sample first reads the kernel process table, then the necessary prefix
of each user area. The user area's owning-slot pointer is checked before
its accounting is accepted. After all user areas have been read, top reads
the relevant process-table prefix again into the same buffer and validates
each row's PID, address and RAM/swap location. This batches the consistency
checks into one final read instead of seeking and reading once per process.
It also avoids allocating a second complete table. An entry that changed
is marked unavailable, and its history is invalidated so it cannot create
a false CPU difference on the next refresh. This is a live observation,
not an atomic snapshot that stops other processes.

The kernel symbols _proc, _nproc, _hz and _cp_time are resolved once from
/unix and read through /dev/kmem. User areas come from /dev/mem or
/dev/swap. sysctl supplies boot time, physical memory and allocation maps;
getloadavg supplies load averages. On this 2.11BSD interface, successful
sysctl calls return a byte count rather than zero.

The login count rereads utmp on every refresh but keeps its file stream
open, as the system top does. Login/logout normally update records in
place, and rewind discards buffered records before rereading. Retaining
the stream avoids repeated open/close operations and buffer allocation.
An initial open failure is retried. If an administrator replaces utmp
with a different file, restart top so it opens the replacement; an open
descriptor continues to refer to the original file.

CPU, memory and process-time fields use integer fixed-point formatting.
Separating the integer part from the remainder before scaling prevents
large counters from overflowing. Quotients are reused rather than asking
the native compiler to repeat a software division for the remainder.
Powers of two use exact shifts and masks, and decimal rounding carries
into the integer part when necessary. Long sampling gaps scale down both
sides of a CPU ratio before multiplication. A forced refresh within the
same kernel clock tick retains the last measured CPU percentage.

The first screen does not wait for a refresh timer: cumulative values are
already meaningful, while rates require two observations. Signals merely
set flags; the main loop performs resizing and cleanup because allocation
and buffered output are unsafe to call from an interrupted signal handler.

Testing and measurement
-----------------------
On a modern development host, run from bsd/top:

    sh tests/run.sh

The tests compile the actual renderer with fake terminal services and
check the resulting characters and attributes. They cover changed spans,
disappearing rows, 200 randomized frames, resizing, allocation failures
and terminal restoration. The actual fixed-point formatter is also tested
against 29 explicit expected results, including rounding boundaries and
the largest unsigned 32-bit counters. Host tests do not replace native
tests of the PDP-11's narrower integer types and library behavior.

Run the same formatter boundary cases on 2.11BSD with:

    /bin/sh tests/native-fixed.sh

The script uses the native compiler and libc, keeps generated files in a
private temporary directory, and cleans up when it finishes. See
tests/README for the distinction between host and native checks.

tests/README.workload describes an opt-in native test with more than 32
occupied processes, a stopped child, a zombie and ongoing process churn.
It has a bounded duration and cleans up its own children.

The native timing harness and methodology are in tests/bench/README.md.
It creates and drains a pseudo-terminal on the PDP, keeping Telnet and
the modern host's network timing outside the measured path. Compare the
same terminal, workload, privileges and refresh interval. Compare stripped
binaries with stripped binaries: debugging symbols increase file length
but do not consume a running process's memory. Observed data + stack and
native child CPU accounting measure different costs from executable size.

PERFORMANCE.md records the current native checks and measurements.
VALIDATION.md preserves the original curses implementation's test report;
its old binary sizes and test counts do not describe this version.

Attribution
-----------
The kernel interfaces and presentation were checked against the installed
2.11BSD ps, vmstat and top sources. The existing UCB top was written by
Digby Tarvin, with later contributions including Johnny Billquist and
other 2.11BSD maintainers. This separate implementation retains that
acknowledgment; its build does not replace the system copy of top.
